    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Opinionated tiny creatures\"—it's just the way I see it! You're right, sometimes the subtle signs can be deceiving, which is why monitoring is crucial. A starter going \"bad\" usually means the balance has tipped so far that undesirable, often smelly, bacteria have taken over, or the environment has become too harsh.\n\nIf it smells overwhelmingly foul—like rotten eggs (hydrogen sulfide) or intensely rancid—that's a serious sign that something is going wrong, not just a flavor profile change. A healthy starter should smell yeasty, slightly acidic, perhaps a bit like yogurt or beer, but not truly foul.\n\nSometimes, a starter just slows down significantly, which isn't necessarily \"bad,\" but rather that it's hungry or too cold. If it's just sluggish but still smells mostly okay, it might just need more food or a warmer spot. If the smell is truly off, it's time to discard most of it and start fresh with a clean batch of flour and water.",
